1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,600
How deep does the security around one card number really go?

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:05,472
Think of a bank vault.

3
00:00:05,522 --> 00:00:07,344
There is a gate on the street.

4
00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,216
A guard inside the door.

5
00:00:09,266 --> 00:00:11,928
Inner doors that open only with the right pass.

6
00:00:11,978 --> 00:00:15,504
A safe at the back, with keys no single person holds.

7
00:00:15,554 --> 00:00:17,256
And cameras everywhere.

8
00:00:17,306 --> 00:00:20,184
No one layer is trusted to stop everything.

9
00:00:20,234 --> 00:00:23,280
Each layer assumes the one before it might fail.

10
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:25,824
That idea is called defence in depth.

11
00:00:25,874 --> 00:00:28,176
The card zone is built the same way.

12
00:00:28,226 --> 00:00:31,272
Follow one card number through it, layer by layer.

13
00:00:31,322 --> 00:00:34,176
Layer one is the gate: the payers' door.

14
00:00:34,226 --> 00:00:38,040
The buyer's browser reaches the card zone only through this door.

15
00:00:38,090 --> 00:00:43,488
It passes the card frame's few requests and answers not found to everything else.

16
00:00:43,538 --> 00:00:46,512
It slows any one buyer who submits too fast.

17
00:00:46,562 --> 00:00:50,400
Layer two is the glass at the counter: the card form's seals.

18
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:53,136
The frame runs only its own two files.

19
00:00:53,186 --> 00:00:55,008
A changed file does not run.

20
00:00:55,058 --> 00:00:57,240
A stranger's script is refused.

21
00:00:57,290 --> 00:01:00,312
Only the payment page its session names may hold it.

22
00:01:00,362 --> 00:01:03,360
This stops a skimmer copying the number as it is typed.

23
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:06,480
Layer three is the guard: the fraud limits.

24
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:14,016
Before any network is asked, the attempt is counted by the card's fingerprint, its first digits, the buyer's address and the shop.

25
00:01:14,066 --> 00:01:17,424
Past the bank's chosen limits, the try is refused.

26
00:01:17,474 --> 00:01:20,352
This stops a thief testing stolen cards.

27
00:01:20,402 --> 00:01:23,448
Layer four is the safe: sealed numbers.

28
00:01:23,498 --> 00:01:27,096
Vault seals the number, and only the sealed value is kept.

29
00:01:27,146 --> 00:01:30,432
The database refuses anything unsealed.

30
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:33,768
The number is opened only to hand it to the card's network.

31
00:01:33,818 --> 00:01:38,280
This stops a stolen database or backup from leaking a single card.

32
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:42,648
Layer five is the inner doors: two proofs on every call.

33
00:01:42,698 --> 00:01:46,440
When ecom asks to charge the card, it never sees the number.

34
00:01:46,490 --> 00:01:48,312
It holds only the token.

35
00:01:48,362 --> 00:01:53,928
Every call it makes carries two proofs: its certificate, and its token from the login server.

36
00:01:53,978 --> 00:01:56,904
Its role allows only the operations it needs.

37
00:01:56,954 --> 00:02:01,368
This stops a stranger, or a caller overstepping, from using the card.

38
00:02:01,418 --> 00:02:05,448
Layer six is the cameras: logs that never show the number.

39
00:02:05,498 --> 00:02:08,088
Every use of card data is written down.

40
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:13,032
Every log line cuts a card number down to its first six and last four digits.

41
00:02:13,082 --> 00:02:15,264
Secrets in a log are starred out.

42
00:02:15,314 --> 00:02:17,496
Alerts carry no card data at all.

43
00:02:17,546 --> 00:02:21,120
This stops the record of what happened from becoming a leak.

44
00:02:21,170 --> 00:02:23,448
Layer seven is the keyholders.

45
00:02:23,498 --> 00:02:25,464
Vault itself starts locked.

46
00:02:25,514 --> 00:02:29,928
To open it, three of five keyholders each bring their own share of the key.

47
00:02:29,978 --> 00:02:32,448
No one person can open the safe alone.

48
00:02:32,498 --> 00:02:35,064
This stops one insider from taking the keys.

49
00:02:35,114 --> 00:02:37,512
Now watch the product, with one buyer.

50
00:02:37,562 --> 00:02:39,504
The buyer opens the payment page.

51
00:02:39,554 --> 00:02:41,376
The padlock shows.

52
00:02:41,426 --> 00:02:44,544
The frame appears and groups the number in fours.

53
00:02:44,594 --> 00:02:48,600
The buyer presses "Send code", and a code arrives on their own phone.

54
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:51,696
They confirm, and the form says "Card accepted".

55
00:02:51,746 --> 00:02:55,752
The page shows only the masked line, the network and the bank.

56
00:02:55,802 --> 00:02:57,864
ecom asks for the charge by token.

57
00:02:57,914 --> 00:02:59,736
The page says "Paid".

58
00:02:59,786 --> 00:03:03,912
Seven layers worked, and the buyer saw two fields and a button.

59
00:03:03,962 --> 00:03:08,256
On the bank's side, the team opens the card zone's logs for that payment.

60
00:03:08,306 --> 00:03:10,848
They see the masked number, never the whole one.

61
00:03:10,898 --> 00:03:13,368
They see who asked for what, and when.

62
00:03:13,418 --> 00:03:15,024
Now Vault restarts.

63
00:03:15,074 --> 00:03:16,896
It comes back locked.

64
00:03:16,946 --> 00:03:23,088
The card form refuses new cards, and an alert says a service the card zone depends on is unavailable.

65
00:03:23,138 --> 00:03:26,352
It stays that way until the keyholders meet and open it.

66
00:03:26,402 --> 00:03:28,224
What does this protect?

67
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:30,552
Any single layer can fail one day.

68
00:03:30,602 --> 00:03:32,424
A script slips past review.

69
00:03:32,474 --> 00:03:34,296
A password leaks.

70
00:03:34,346 --> 00:03:36,456
A copy of the database escapes.

71
00:03:36,506 --> 00:03:39,792
When one layer fails, the next one still holds.

72
00:03:39,842 --> 00:03:42,912
That protects cardholders, shops and the bank.

73
00:03:42,962 --> 00:03:44,784
Back to the vault.

74
00:03:44,834 --> 00:03:48,912
Gate, guard, inner doors, safe, keyholders and cameras.

75
00:03:48,962 --> 00:03:51,288
A thief must beat every one of them.

76
00:03:51,338 --> 00:03:53,160
Each one only has to hold.

77
00:03:53,210 --> 00:03:55,440
Next: how a merchant signs up.
